To use brands in your project, make sure you import the required brand themes into your main css file.

```html
<StaticCode language="css" code={`
@import '@westpac/style-config/themes/wbc';
@import '@westpac/style-config/themes/wbcLegacy';
@import '@westpac/style-config/themes/stg';
`} />
```

Add the custom attribute tag `data-brand="brand_name"` to your HTML tag or parent container. You can use the following valid brand names:

- `wbc`: Westpac (refreshed appearance, previously WBC26; the default selection)
- `wbcLegacy`: Westpac Legacy (previous Westpac appearance)
- `stg`: St.George Bank
- `bom`: Bank of Melbourne
- `bsa`: BankSA

**Breaking change:** applications currently using `wbc` must switch to `wbcLegacy` to retain their previous appearance. Change the theme import to `@westpac/style-config/themes/wbcLegacy`, set `data-brand="wbcLegacy"`, and update explicit component brand props such as `Header` and `Footer` to `brand="wbcLegacy"`. Update saved brand selections and custom `wbc:` Tailwind variants to `wbcLegacy:` too.

The public `wbc26` identifier has been removed without a compatibility alias. Existing WBC26 consumers must use `wbc` instead, including imports, attributes, props and custom variants. Per-brand W3C, standalone CSS, Swift and Android outputs follow the same mapping; update their paths and generated identifiers. Aggregate token keys retain their Figma source names: `Westpac 2026` supplies `wbc`, and `Westpac` supplies `wbcLegacy`.

Both Westpac brands support light and dark tokens. Review both modes and update visual snapshots deliberately after migrating. Existing STG, BSA and BOM values are unchanged. Icons now support dedicated colour tokens across all brands, and Westpac pictogram colours have been updated.

```html
<StaticCode language="jsx" code={`
<div data-brand="wbc">
  <!-- Your app content -->
</div>
`} />
```

For multibrand applications use the `data-brand` attribute to dynamically switch between brands

```html
<StaticCode language="jsx" code={`
<div data-brand={currentBrand}>
  <!-- Your app content -->
</div>
`} />
```
